    "result_text": "I've implemented `build_context` in `app/context.py`. The existing tests pass (4 of 4), and a quick manual check shows it behaves as you described, but I didn't add any new tests for it.\n\n**How it works:**\n- It looks up the documents with `retriever.invoke(question, config={\"tags\": [TRACE_TAG]})`. That's the standard way to call any LangChain retriever, so it works the same for `KeywordRetriever` and a vector-store retriever. Passing the tag in the config puts `kb-context` on the traced lookup.\n- It goes through the results in the order the retriever returned them and skips any document whose `metadata[\"source\"]` has already been used. It stops after `MAX_SOURCES` entries.\n- Each entry is `[n] <source>` on one line, then the document text with surrounding whitespace removed. Entries are joined by one blank line. If nothing is found, the result is `\"\"`.\n\n**Manual check:** I ran it on five matching documents, two of them from the same source (`a.md`, `a.md`, `c.md`, `d.md`, `e.md`):\n- The output was `'[1] a.md\\nvpn a\\n\\n[2] c.md\\nvpn c\\n\\n[3] d.md\\nvpn d'`: the duplicate was dropped, it stopped at 3 entries, and the text was trimmed.\n- The traced lookup carried the tag `['kb-context']`.\n- A question that matched nothing returned `''`.\n\n**Decision for you:** if a production document has no `\"source\"` in its metadata, it's listed as `[n] None`, and later documents without a source are dropped as duplicates of it. If your vector store might return documents like that, you may want a different rule for them.\n\nI couldn't delete my throwaway check script, `_check.py`, because the `rm` wasn't approved. Please delete it from the project root."
  },
